[center][b][size=4]This is a 1.0 candidate in active testing. If you play it, PLEASE send feedback or bug reports, and include the MFO.log from that session[/size][/b]
[i](Documents\My Games\Skyrim Special Edition\SKSE\MFO.log)[/i][/center]
[line]

Programmable follower behaviour: every follower carries ordered lists of [i]Gambits[/i] — [b][Condition] -> [Action][/b] rules, first match wins — authored live, in game, per follower. Final Fantasy XII's gambit system rebuilt on Skyrim's own actor primitives. Followers cast (their own AI, animated, at chosen targets), attack, drink, restock, loot, [i]walk[/i] to loot, and trade at merchants — selling their junk and buying the supplies they run short on. Nothing is scripted to a specific follower: it works on anyone your framework recognises as a teammate, vanilla or modded.

Built entirely on Linux with no Creation Kit and no xEdit: the plugin is generated byte-by-byte from Python, the install-time patcher is a Mutagen/.NET (Synthesis) tool, and the SKSE plugin builds on GitHub Actions.

[size=5][b]Features[/b][/size]

[b]Two tables, not one[/b] — each follower carries independent [i]combat[/i] and [i]logistics[/i] rule lists with separate slots; they never interleave. Top-down, first match wins, one action per tick — a follower cannot heal and attack in the same cycle, so the cost of a heal is the attack not made. The list is yours: no reordering, no deduplication, no coordination between followers.

[b]Followers loot like people, not vacuums[/b] — they [i]walk[/i] to loot rather than teleport-grabbing across the room, pick locks they're skilled enough for, and leave gear and gold on a fresh corpse for a few seconds first — your pick. They never take owned goods, hold off entirely while you're sneaking, and never loot inside a player home unless you opt in. Equipment upgrades are judged by category and metric, never by item — an archer won't swap to a mace, a light-armour follower won't pick up plate — so modded gear works with no patch. Magic users dress like mages: a follower with cast gambits prefers enchanted apparel that boosts his most-cast school (a Destruction robe over a higher-rated helmet, the finer robe among matches) and keeps one backup dagger for when his magicka runs dry.

[b]Followers trade at merchants[/b] — stand a follower next to a vendor and they sell their unworn junk (highest value first, capped by the vendor's own gold) and buy the supplies they've run short on, chosen from what that vendor [i]actually stocks[/i], from their own purse. Off by default; enable [b]Follower economy[/b] in the MCM.

[b]Confidence, on an invisible string[/b] — how far a follower operates from you is a live readout of how confident they are to survive alone right now. Healthy in an easy zone, they push ahead and range out to loot; hurt, or in a hard fight, they pull back and fight at your side — badly outmatched, they fall back to you on their own. You never see a number; you read it through behaviour.

[b]Reactions are human, not robotic[/b] — a gambit decision is a choice reaction, which in people runs 300–600 ms, so responses are drawn from a right-skewed distribution rather than firing on a metronome. Followers occasionally miss a beat. Higher Rapport ranks visibly hesitate less.

[b]Progression through Rapport[/b] — rule slots are earned by fighting alongside that [i]specific[/i] follower. It's never pooled or transferable, and it survives dismissal: leave someone in Breezehome for two hundred hours and they're exactly as you left them. What a follower [i]can[/i] be told to do depends on their own skills and spellbook — a battlemage opens cast actions a pure warrior never will.

[b]The Field Orders board[/b] — rules are authored in game, per follower, on the Field Orders power: a native menu (four skins, full controller support) with a Followers roster, the Gambits editor, and a plain-language summary of every rule. A passive HUD shows per-follower rank, rapport, live vitals, and distance while you fight. Settings live in an MCM, including the Behaviour Layer page for the confidence leash and walk-to-loot.

[b]Layered on vanilla AI, never replacing it[/b] — a follower with no matching rule behaves byte-identically to one without MFO installed: no no-op package, no neutral command, no engine call at all. MFO drives followers through runtime AI, not record edits, so it does not conflict with follower mods, AI overhauls, or combat mods.

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]SKSE64[/*]
[*]Address Library for SKSE Plugins[/*]
[*]SkyUI[/*]
[*]MCM Helper (version 9 or newer)[/*]
[*]powerofthree's Papyrus Extender — required [i]only[/i] for the optional follower economy (selling/buying); everything else runs without it.[/*]
[*][url=https://github.com/Mutagen-Modding/Synthesis]Synthesis[/url] — MFO ships a Synthesis patcher that builds an item catalog from your load order so gambit categories (potions, ammo, gear classes) cover modded items with no per-mod patch (see Install). Synthesis carries its own .NET requirement, so there's nothing extra to install for MFO.[/*]
[*]No DLC required.[/*]
[/list]
[b]Anniversary Edition (1.6.x) is recommended:[/b] the [i]walk-to-it[/i] behaviours — walking to loot, Flee to player, and Auto-retreat — ride a native alias fill that is only verified on AE, so on Special Edition (1.5.97) they are inactive (loot is picked up at arm's reach; flee/retreat do nothing). Everything else — combat gambits, restocking, looting itself, and the merchant economy — works on SE and AE alike. VR is not supported.

Developed and tested on Skyrim SE 1.6.1170 against a Requiem-based list (LoreRim) and a pure-vanilla Steam Deck install. The bundled MFO.dll is part of the mod and required.

[size=5][b]Install[/b][/size]

[b]1.[/b] Install the package with your mod manager as a normal mod, and enable [b]MFO.esp[/b]. Load order is not sensitive — MFO drives followers through runtime AI, not record edits.

[b]2.[/b] (Recommended) Adapt MFO's item catalog to your load order with [b]Synthesis[/b] (there's no exe to run):
[list]
[*][b]Easiest:[/b] download the [b]MFO.synth[/b] file (in Optional Files), then in Synthesis select a group and double-click it — it adds the MFO patcher for you, with the correct project already selected.[/*]
[*][b]Or manually:[/b] add a Git Repository patcher pointing to [url=https://github.com/marthofdoom/MFO]github.com/marthofdoom/MFO[/url], and select the project [i]installer/MFO.Synthesis/MFO.Synthesis.csproj[/i].[/*]
[/list]
Run your Synthesis pipeline, and re-run it after any load-order change. Without it MFO is still fully playable — it falls back to a shipped catalog; the patcher upgrades gambit item coverage to your actual list.

[b]3.[/b] Launch through SKSE. Open the MCM ([b]Mod Configuration -> marth's Follower Overhaul[/b]) to configure; every behaviour ships off or conservative by default.

No FOMOD — the options are runtime MCM toggles. [b]Updating mid-playthrough is safe:[/b] all state lives in the SKSE co-save and reverts cleanly on load.

[size=5][b]FAQ[/b][/size]

[b]Will it conflict with my follower framework / AI overhaul / combat mod?[/b]
No. MFO layers on top of whatever manages your followers — it acts on anyone flagged as your teammate, through runtime AI. It edits no follower records, no AI packages of other mods, and a follower with no matching rule is byte-identical to one without MFO.

[b]Does it work with modded followers and modded items?[/b]
Yes. Followers are detected by teammate status, not by a list. Item categories (potions, ammo, gear classes) come from the Synthesis-built catalog of your load order, so modded gear is judged by the same class-and-metric rules with no per-mod patch.

[b]Do I have to run Synthesis?[/b]
It's recommended but not mandatory. Without it MFO uses a shipped item catalog; with it the catalog is rebuilt from the mods you actually run.

[b]Why won't my follower cast the spell I set?[/b]
Competence is not permission: if they lack the magicka or the skill, the rule fails and the next one is tried. The board tells you why, so a list that doesn't work is legible rather than mysterious.

[b]Can I turn parts off or tune it?[/b]
Yes. Everything ships off or conservative; looting, the economy, auto-retreat, the confidence leash, dibs timing, and the rest are MCM toggles and sliders.

[size=5][b]Roadmap[/b][/size]

The core loop is here: gambits on both tables, human-feel actuation, looting, the economy, Rapport, and the board. Current directions:

[b]Town errands[/b] — followers autonomously walking to merchants and doors on their own business, the walk-to-loot machinery generalised.

[b]Vocabulary by rank[/b] — gating [i]which[/i] conditions and actions a follower can be taught (not just slot count) behind Rapport.

[b]Deeper interop[/b] — compatibility contracts with follower levelling and equipment-visibility mods as their APIs allow.

